.TH SYS_PROC_CMDLINE 2 "May 2026" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_proc_cmdline \- query process argument vector
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysinfo.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_proc_cmdline(pid_t " pid ", char **" argv ", size_t *" argc ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sys_proc_cmdline ()
copies out the cmdline_tail blob captured at exec for process
.IR pid .
The blob is a flat byte sequence in which each argument string is
NUL-terminated, mirroring the Linux
.B /proc/PID/cmdline
layout.  Userland tools (ps, /proc helpers) split on NUL to
reconstruct the argument array.
.PP
The
.I argv
parameter is reinterpreted as a flat
.B char *
buffer of size
.I *argc
bytes.  On entry
.I *argc
is the buffer capacity; on return it is the true blob size in
bytes.  If the user buffer was too small, the kernel writes what
fits and the caller can re-allocate based on
.I *argc
and retry.
.PP
.I pid
= 0 means the calling process.  Probe with
.I argv
=
.B NULL ,
.I *argc
= 0.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, a negated errno on failure.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ESRCH
.I pid
does not refer to an existing process.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I argv
or
.I argc
points outside the caller's address space.
.SH NOTES
The kernel does not reconstruct the per-argument
.B char **
pointer array — the on-disk blob layout is the API.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR execve (2),
.BR sys_proc_environ (2),
.BR sys_proc_info (2)
